ENVIRONMENTAL SUSTAINABILITY OS PROJECT

OVERVIEW

This project extends xv6, a Unix-like operating system used for educational purposes, 
with environmental sustainability features. The system monitors and manages pollution 
levels across different campus zones and tracks energy consumption of processes.

The implementation includes:
  - Environment tracking: Monitor pollution levels in 5 different zones
  - Energy accounting: Track CPU energy consumption by individual processes
  - Sensor simulation: Processes that generate pollution data
  - Mitigation: Autonomous processes that reduce pollution when thresholds are exceeded
  - Status monitoring: Real-time display of environmental metrics

FEATURES

New System Calls:
  - update_env(zone, delta): Update pollution level for a campus zone
  - get_env(zone): Get current pollution level for a zone
  - get_energy(pid): Get accumulated energy ticks consumed by a process
  - set_zone(zone, is_mitigator): Register a process with a zone (flag for mitigators)

New User Programs:
  - sensor: Simulates environmental pollution (adds pollution periodically)
  - monitor: Displays real-time status of all 5 campus zones
  - mitigator: Automatically reduces pollution when levels exceed thresholds
  - energy_test: Tests energy accounting functionality
  - env_test: Tests environment update functionality

Kernel Modifications:
  - Extended process structure to track zone assignments
  - Per-zone pollution tracking in kernel state
  - Energy accounting for process scheduling
  - Protection for mitigator processes (cannot be throttled)

BUILDING AND RUNNING

To build xv6 on an x86 ELF machine (like Linux), run:
  make

On non-x86 or non-ELF machines (like OS X or Windows), you will need to install 
a cross-compiler gcc suite capable of producing x86 ELF binaries 
(see https://pdos.csail.mit.edu/6.828/).

Then run:
  make TOOLPREFIX=i386-jos-elf-

Install QEMU PC simulator and run:
  make qemu

USING THE ENVIRONMENTAL SUSTAINABILITY FEATURES

1. Start the monitor to view pollution levels:
   $ monitor

2. In other shell instances, start sensors for different zones:
   $ sensor 0    # Zone 0 sensor
   $ sensor 1    # Zone 1 sensor

3. Start mitigators to manage pollution:
   $ mitigator 0   # Zone 0 mitigator
   $ mitigator 1   # Zone 1 mitigator

4. Test the energy accounting system:
   $ energy_test

5. Test the environment syscalls:
   $ env_test

The monitor will display pollution levels and mark zones with [CRITICAL] status 
when pollution exceeds 100 units. Mitigators will automatically reduce pollution 
by 50 units when cleaning a zone.

ARCHITECTURE NOTES

The system uses a simple model where:
  - 5 campus zones (0-4) can track independent pollution levels
  - Processes can associate with a zone to generate or mitigate pollution
  - Mitigators are protected from throttling to ensure cleanup operations
  - Energy accounting is performed during context switches

